{
 "ep": 19,
 "total": 140,
 "title": "Create Read-Only Users Correctly",
 "phase": "Installation & Administration",
 "lines": [
  {
   "say": "A read-only PostgreSQL user needs more than one GRANT.",
   "cap": "Read-only needs *more than one GRANT.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "This is episode nineteen of the PostgreSQL DBA series. Today: a read-only user, built correctly.",
   "cap": "Episode 19: a *read-only user,* done right.",
   "cue": "read-only"
  },
  {
   "say": "Read-only means the user can connect and read your data, but cannot change it.",
   "cap": "Connect and *read.* Never *change.*",
   "cue": "read"
  },
  {
   "say": "That takes four layers of privileges: the database, the schema, the tables, and the sequences.",
   "cap": "Four layers: *database, schema, tables, sequences.*",
   "cue": "database"
  },
  {
   "say": "Start with the role: CREATE ROLE ro_user with LOGIN, and set its password with psql's backslash password command.",
   "cap": "*CREATE ROLE ro_user LOGIN,* password via *\\password*",
   "cue": "password"
  },
  {
   "say": "Layer one: grant connect on the database. Public has it by default, but an explicit grant survives a revoke from public.",
   "cap": "Layer 1: *CONNECT* on the database.",
   "cue": "explicit"
  },
  {
   "say": "Layer two: grant usage on the schema. Without it, the user cannot even look up the tables inside.",
   "cap": "Layer 2: *USAGE* on the schema.",
   "cue": "Without"
  },
  {
   "say": "Layer three: grant SELECT on all tables in schema app. This also covers views and foreign tables.",
   "cap": "Layer 3: *SELECT* on all tables, views too.",
   "cue": "views"
  },
  {
   "say": "Layer four: grant SELECT on all sequences. That allows reading them and currval, but not nextval.",
   "cap": "Layer 4: *SELECT* on sequences, no *nextval.*",
   "cue": "currval"
  },
  {
   "say": "Now we test, connected as ro_user. SELECT from orders returns both rows.",
   "cap": "Test as ro_user: *SELECT works.*",
   "cue": "SELECT"
  },
  {
   "say": "INSERT fails with permission denied for table orders.",
   "cap": "INSERT: *permission denied.*",
   "cue": "INSERT"
  },
  {
   "say": "CREATE TABLE fails too, with permission denied for schema app.",
   "cap": "CREATE TABLE: *denied* on schema app.",
   "cue": "CREATE"
  },
  {
   "say": "nextval is refused on the sequence, but reading its last value works.",
   "cap": "*nextval* refused, *last_value* readable.",
   "cue": "reading"
  },
  {
   "say": "The classic trap: on all tables covers only tables that exist now. Tomorrow's tables are not included.",
   "cap": "Trap: *ALL TABLES* means *existing* tables only.",
   "cue": "Tomorrow"
  },
  {
   "say": "The fix is alter default privileges. It sets privileges for objects created in the future.",
   "cap": "Fix: *ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES* for future objects.",
   "cue": "future"
  },
  {
   "say": "But it only applies to objects created by the role named in for role, or by the current role if you leave it out.",
   "cap": "Only for objects made by the *FOR ROLE* role.",
   "cue": "current"
  },
  {
   "say": "Our test: app_owner created payments, and ro_user can read it. postgres created refunds: permission denied.",
   "cap": "payments *readable,* refunds *denied.*",
   "cue": "refunds"
  },
  {
   "say": "A common practice: set default privileges for every role that creates tables.",
   "cap": "Set defaults for *every role that creates tables.*",
   "cue": "every"
  },
  {
   "say": "Next trap: by default, public has the temporary privilege on databases, so ro_user can still create temporary tables.",
   "cap": "Trap 2: *TEMPORARY* is granted to PUBLIC.",
   "cue": "temporary"
  },
  {
   "say": "To block that, revoke temporary on the database from public. It affects every role, so grant it back where needed.",
   "cap": "*REVOKE TEMPORARY* affects *every role.*",
   "cue": "affects"
  },
  {
   "say": "Also, default_transaction_read_only is not a security control. The user can simply SET it back to off.",
   "cap": "*default_transaction_read_only* is *not security.*",
   "cue": "SET"
  },
  {
   "say": "The predefined role pg_read_all_data reads all tables, views and sequences. Grant it with care.",
   "cap": "*pg_read_all_data* reads *everything.* Use with care.",
   "cue": "care"
  },
  {
   "say": "Recap: connect on the database, usage on the schema, SELECT on tables and sequences.",
   "cap": "Recap: *CONNECT, USAGE, SELECT.*",
   "cue": "usage"
  },
  {
   "say": "Then default privileges for every role that creates tables, and always test as the user itself.",
   "cap": "Plus *default privileges,* then *test.*",
   "cue": "test"
  },
  {
   "say": "Next up: GRANT, REVOKE and default privileges in depth. Follow so you don't miss it, and send this to a friend who's learning PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "Next up: *GRANT and REVOKE.* *Follow* and share."
  }
 ],
